{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KRHNsqWsrgi5tqb7uMQirt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P4BXChci23qFpkMsGSnMGk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper: \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies schema complexity degradation in training-free LLM IE. It hypothesizes two degradation channels: (1) type count → recall degradation, (2) type confusability → type confusion. It develops SDGD (Schema DAG-Guided Decomposition) with variants (zero, cal, v2) and tests across 3 IE tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), 3 embeddings (Qwen3, LLaMA, MiniLM).\n\n### Key findings (all negative):\n1. Confusability predicts Qwen3 errors (r=0.156) but not LLaMA's (r=0.018) → model-specific, not universal\n2. SDGD-zero consistently worse than random batching (Δ=-0.061 F1, p=0.003 over 5 seeds)\n3. SDGD-cal marginal improvement over zero but still below single-pass (-16.7 F1) and random (-4.2)\n4. SDGD-v2 improved merge reduces conflicts 28%→19% but still -14.7 F1 vs single-pass\n5. Per-type splitting: highest recall (0.586) but lowest precision (0.194), 42% merge conflicts\n6. 2×2 factorial: count and BCS main effects directionally correct but interaction non-significant (F=0.42, p=0.52)\n7. DocRED: count channel generalizes (-44pp) but confusability channel nearly vanishes (4-5pp BCS delta)\n8. SCS proxy validation: best ρ=0.18, explains ~3% variance, top-50 recall only 0.28\n\n### Key tables:\n- Table 1: 2×2 factorial (Low/High Count × Low/High BCS) F1 values\n- Table 2: ANOVA (Count p=0.013, BCS p=0.054, interaction p=0.52)\n- Table 3: Cross-model pairwise SCS-confusion correlation\n- Table 4: Cross-model comparison of confusability effects\n- Table 5: LLaMA count curve (non-monotonic F1)\n- Table 6: SCS vs empirical confusion (ρ=0.05-0.18)\n- Table 7: Full decomposition comparison on Few-NERD (7 modes)\n- Table 8: 5-seed SDGD-zero vs random\n- Table 9: MAVEN decomposition results\n- Table 10: DocRED count curve\n- Table 11: DocRED BCS-controlled accuracy\n\n### Experiment coverage reference\nThe full experimental design (from review_context.yaml) includes these imagined experiments:\n1. DocRED Confusability Study (exp-005) — covered in paper §4.6\n2. Count × Confusability Interaction (exp-006) — covered in §4.2\n3. SDGD-zero on MAVEN (exp-009) — covered in §4.5.2\n4. SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (exp-010) — covered in §4.5.1\n5. SDGD-cal on MAVEN (exp-011) — covered in §4.5.2\n6. LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation (exp-012) — covered in §4.3\n7. Random Batching Ablation (exp-013) — covered in §4.5.1\n8. Per-Type Splitting (exp-014) — covered in §4.5.1\n9. SCS Proxy Validation (exp-015) — covered in §4.4\n10. SDGD v2 on Few-NERD (exp-008-v2) — covered in §4.5.1\n\n## Your review task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on experimental design, methodology rigor, statistical validity)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps, experimental limitations, statistical concerns, missing controls)\n4. **Detailed questions/comments** on specific experimental choices, statistical methodology, and results interpretation\n5. **Experiment coverage assessment**: Are the imagined experiments adequately covered? Any notable gaps?\n6. **Score** (1-6) with **confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Pay attention to:\n- Statistical rigor (effect sizes, significance tests, multiple comparisons)\n- Experimental controls and confounds\n- Whether the conclusions are supported by the evidence\n- Adequacy of baselines\n- Reproducibility concerns\n- Whether the \"negative results\" framing is justified or could mask methodological issues\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35120,"cache_read_input_tokens":22933,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":35120},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e","uuid":"4f0d315f-68e7-41cd-b577-f8bdc68b9079"}
