luation\n3. Lu et al. 2023 - Chameleon\n4. Zhou et al. 2024 - UniNER\n5. Rogers et al. 2020 - BERTology primer\n6. Liu et al. 2024 - Lost in the Middle\n\n## Your review task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty of contributions, positioning, significance for the field)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty concerns, related work gaps, positioning issues, contribution significance)\n4. **Detailed comments** on:\n   - Novelty assessment: Are the negative findings genuinely surprising or somewhat expected?\n   - Related work completeness: What important prior work is missing?\n   - Contribution significance: Do the negative results constitute sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n   - Framing: Is \"negative results\" the right frame, or does it mask insufficient method development?\n   - Positioning within the broader LLM IE literature\n5. **Score** (1-6) with **confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Whether the related work section adequately covers the landscape (schema decomposition methods, LLM-based IE beyond GPT-NER, structured prediction with LLMs, prompt engineering for complex schemas, etc.)\n- Whether the novelty claims are overstated or understated\n- Whether the \"methodological contribution\" framing (multi-channel degradation, embedding ablation, merge amplification) is substantive\n- Whether a reviewer would see this as \"they tried something obvious, it didn't work\" vs \"they systematically ruled out a promising direction\"\n- The paper has only 6 references — is this adequate?\n","uuid":"d484b6ed-e5a0-416f-817a-b595fb7587bc","session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper: \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies schema complexity degradation in training-free LLM IE. It hypothesizes two degradation channels: type count (→recall) and type confusability (→type confusion). It develops SDGD (Schema DAG-Guided Decomposition) using graph coloring based on embedding similarity, with variants: SDGD-zero (embedding SCS), SDGD-cal (empirical calibration), SDGD-v2 (improved merge). Tests on 3 tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), 3 embeddings.\n\n### Three main negative findings:\n1. Confusability is model-specific (Qwen3 r=0.156 vs LLaMA r=0.018), not a universal schema property\n2. SDGD consistently underperforms single-pass and even random batching (Δ=-0.061 F1, p=0.003)\n3. Merge amplification is structural: even improved merge (SDGD-v2) leaves -14.7 F1 gap; per-type splitting shows 42% merge conflicts\n\n### Related work section (§2, brief):\n- Training-free LLM IE: GPT-NER (Wang et al. 2023), Li et al. 2023, UniNER (Zhou et al. 2024), LLM-PEE\n- Schema decomposition: \"Prior methods operate on unvalidated assumptions\"\n- Negative results in NLP: Rogers et al. 2020, \"Lost in the Middle\" (Liu et al. 2024)\n\n### Paper structure:\n- 9 pages, 11 tables, claims to release code/data\n- Positions as \"negative results that reframe assumptions\"\n- Targets EMNLP Main / Findings\n\n### References (only 6 total):\n1. Wang et al. 2023 - GPT-NER\n2. Li et al. 2023 - ChatGPT IE evaluation\n3. Lu et al. 2023 - Chameleon\n4. Zhou et al. 2024 - UniNER\n5. Rogers et al. 2020 - BERTology primer\n6. Liu et al. 2024 - Lost in the Middle\n\n## Your review task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty of contributions, positioning, significance for the field)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty concerns, related work gaps, positioning issues, contribution significance)\n4. **Detailed comments** on:\n   - Novelty assessment: Are the negative findings genuinely surprising or somewhat expected?\n   - Related work completeness: What important prior work is missing?\n   - Contribution significance: Do the negative results constitute sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n   - Framing: Is \"negative results\" the right frame, or does it mask insufficient method development?\n   - Positioning within the broader LLM IE literature\n5. **Score** (1-6) with **confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Whether the related work section adequately covers the landscape (schema decomposition methods, LLM-based IE beyond GPT-NER, structured prediction with LLMs, prompt engineering for complex schemas, etc.)\n- Whether the novelty claims are overstated or understated\n- Whether the \"methodological contribution\" framing (multi-channel degradation, embedding ablation, merge amplification) is substantive\n- Whether a reviewer would see this as \"they tried something obvious, it didn't work\" vs \"they systematically ruled out a promising direction\"\n- The paper has only 6 references — is this adequate?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKFYhuRUbBXtLSeGa24Z8G","session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e","uuid":"5a1e873f-e77a-4ebf-9190-6d7147450fec","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:52:18.483Z"}
