{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P4BXChci23qFpkMsGSnMGk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a complete picture. Let me now compose my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments Focus)**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas, hypothesizing two independent channels (type count causing recall loss, type confusability causing type confusion). The authors develop SDGD, a schema decomposition framework based on graph coloring over pairwise type similarity, tested across three IE tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), two LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and three embedding spaces. The findings are uniformly negative: confusability is model-specific (significant for Qwen3 but null for LLaMA), all SDGD variants underperform single-pass baselines, and embedding-based confusability proxies are too weak (rho <= 0.18) to guide decomposition.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Rigorous controlled experimental design.** The 2x2 factorial (count x BCS), the systematic variation across 3 tasks, 2 models, and 3 embeddings, and the multi-level analysis (pairwise, per-type, batch) demonstrate commendable methodological discipline. The controlled batch construction with fixed count and varying BCS is a clean experimental design for isolating confusability.\n\n- **Appropriate statistical methodology for the factorial analysis.** The ANOVA with effect sizes (eta-squared), paired t-tests with 5 seeds for SDGD-zero vs. random, and reporting of both Pearson and Spearman correlations are sound. The significance levels are properly reported.\n\n- **Transparent reporting of negative results.** The paper does not bury the failures. The 5-seed SDGD-zero vs. random comparison (Table 8) with 100% consistency and p=0.003 is a well-designed ablation. The full decomposition spectrum (Table 7) from single-pass through per-type splitting provides comprehensive coverage.\n\n- **Mechanistic diagnosis.** The merge amplification analysis -- showing that precision loss has a structural component beyond merge conflicts (estimated ~0.35 F1 even with perfect merge oracle) -- is a useful insight for the field.\n\n- **Multi-model validation is attempted honestly.** Rather than cherry-picking the positive Qwen3 result, the authors confront the LLaMA null head-on, testing all three embeddings to rule out embedding choice as an explanation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"negative result\" framing may mask methodological deficiencies in the merge strategy.** The paper's core claim that merge amplification is \"structural\" and \"irreducible\" rests on testing only naive union merge, confidence-weighted dedup, and type-priority resolution. These are three relatively simple merge strategies. The claim that \"even a hypothetical perfect merge oracle would yield ~0.35 F1\" is stated but not formally derived -- it appears to be an extrapolation from the observation that merge conflicts account for ~40% of precision loss. More sophisticated approaches (e.g., LLM-based re-ranking of conflicting predictions, iterative multi-pass refinement, or constrained decoding) are acknowledged in Limitations but dismissed without testing. Calling the bottleneck \"fundamental\" after testing three simple strategies overstates the evidence.\n\n- **Confound between batch size and merge difficulty.** SDGD uses k=8 batches for 66 types (average ~8 types/batch), while random batching uses the same k. But SDGD's graph coloring may produce highly unbalanced batches (the project records show batches of [18,12,11,7,7,5,3,3] before v2 min_batch_size enforcement). Small batches (3 types) over-predict 4.4x while large batches (18 types) under-predict 0.6x. The paper does not adequately control for batch size distribution -- SDGD-zero's inferior performance may be driven by its unbalanced batch sizes rather than by confusability-aware grouping being harmful per se.\n\n- **The LLaMA null result has alternative explanations that are under-explored.** The paper attributes LLaMA's null confusability signal to a fundamentally different error profile (omission vs. substitution). But LLaMA-3.3-70B is a 70B model vs. Qwen3's 32B -- the model size difference alone could explain better type discrimination. The claim that this reveals \"model-specificity of confusability\" would be much stronger if tested on another 32B model (e.g., LLaMA-3.3-32B or a similar-scale model). As is, the paper cannot distinguish \"model family effect\" from \"model scale effect.\"\n\n- **The SCS proxy validation (Table 6) numbers are suspiciously low compared to the pairwise correlation results.** The paper reports pairwise SCS-confusion r=0.156 (p<1e-11) for Qwen3 on Few-NERD but SCS proxy rho=0.18. If SCS and confusion have r=0.156 in Pearson terms, the Spearman rho should be comparable or higher (Spearman is more robust to outliers). The jump from r=0.156 (Table 3) to rho=0.18 (Table 6) is small but plausible; however, the framing is inconsistent -- the pairwise correlation is presented as \"significant evidence for confusability\" while the nearly identical number is presented as \"explains only ~3% of variance\" when evaluating the proxy. This rhetorical asymmetry undermines trust.\n\n- **BCS effect in the factorial is borderline and inconsistently interpreted.** BCS main effect F=3.90, p=0.054 (Table 2) is not significant at alpha=0.05. The paper acknowledges this but still treats confusability as a validated channel. In a negative-results paper, this borderline result deserves more careful treatment -- is confusability even a real signal on Qwen3 at the batch level, or only at the pairwise level?\n\n- **No correction for multiple comparisons.** The paper tests pairwise correlations across 3 embeddings x 2 models x 2+ datasets, plus per-type correlations, batch-level effects, and factorial effects. No multiple-comparison correction (Bonferroni, FDR) is applied. Given the number of tests, some \"significant\" results may be false positives. This is particularly concerning for the LLaMA analysis where 6+ tests are run and all are null -- the absence of correction cuts both ways.\n\n- **DocRED pairwise correlation (r=0.038, p=0.082) is borderline non-significant but treated as definitively null.** With n=4,560 pairs, even small effects should be detectable. The paper interprets this as \"confusability channel nearly vanishes\" on document-level RE, but the confidence interval on r likely includes values up to ~0.06-0.07. A more measured conclusion would note that the effect may exist but is smaller than on sentence-level tasks.\n\n- **Limited evaluation metrics.** The paper uses F1/precision/recall for NER/ED and accuracy for RE. No evaluation of entity detection (span-level) vs. entity classification (type-level) separately, which would directly test the multi-channel model. The count channel should manifest in span detection; the confusability channel should manifest in type classification given correct spans. This decomposed evaluation is crucial for the two-channel claim but absent.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Detailed Questions and Comments\n\n**Q1 (Batch construction methodology):** How are \"low BCS\" and \"high BCS\" batches constructed? If by extremal selection (top/bottom quartile of possible batches), the effect sizes are inflated relative to what SDGD would encounter on average. The paper should report the BCS distribution of random batches and where the selected batches fall.\n\n**Q2 (SDGD-zero batch composition):** The project records reveal that SDGD-zero produced batches of [18,12,11,7,7,5,3,3] types, with 3-type batches over-predicting 4.4x. Was the v2 min_batch_size=8 constraint applied before the results in Table 7? If not, the SDGD-zero failure is substantially attributable to degenerate batch sizes rather than confusability-aware grouping being harmful. This distinction is critical for the paper's central negative claim.\n\n**Q3 (ANOVA cell sizes):** How many batches per cell in the 2x2 factorial? If each cell has only 5 random batches (as suggested by the DocRED count curve design), the ANOVA has very low power. The non-significant interaction (F=0.42, p=0.52) cannot distinguish \"no interaction\" from \"insufficient power to detect interaction.\"\n\n**Q4 (LLaMA non-monotonic F1):** The LLaMA count curve shows F1 rising from 0.275 (count=22) to 0.395 (count=44). This is a striking non-monotonicity that is explained as \"precision-recall tradeoff\" but deserves deeper investigation. Does the model switch extraction strategies at different prompt lengths? Are there ceiling effects in the prompt format?\n\n**Q5 (Graph coloring threshold):** The threshold tau=0.8 is mentioned but no sensitivity analysis is presented. Was this chosen to optimize SDGD's performance or fixed a priori? If optimized, the negative results are stronger; if fixed a priori, SDGD may have failed due to a poor threshold choice.\n\n**Q6 (Calibration data for SDGD-cal):** With only 5 examples per type and 66 types, most type pairs will have zero co-occurrences in the calibration set. How is the empirical confusion matrix computed for pairs with no calibration co-occurrence? If assumed to be zero, the matrix is extremely sparse and the claim that \"empirical calibration cannot overcome merge amplification\" is confounded by insufficient calibration data.\n\n**Q7 (Sample size for evaluation):** How many test instances are used for each evaluation? The paper does not report dataset splits or test set sizes. With small test sets, the F1 differences could be within sampling noise.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 10 imagined experiments from the review context are covered in the paper:\n\n| Experiment | Section | Coverage |\n|---|---|---|\n| exp-005 (DocRED) | S4.6 | Adequate - count curve and BCS-controlled results reported |\n| exp-006 (2x2 Factorial) | S4.2 | Adequate - ANOVA with effect sizes |\n| exp-009 (SDGD-zero MAVEN) | S4.5.2 | Adequate |\n| exp-010 (SDGD-cal Few-NERD) | S4.5.1 | Adequate |\n| exp-011 (SDGD-cal MAVEN) | S4.5.2 | Adequate |\n| exp-012 (LLaMA validation) | S4.3 | **Strong** - comprehensive multi-embedding, multi-level |\n| exp-013 (Random ablation) | S4.5.1 | Adequate - 5-seed comparison |\n| exp-014 (Per-type splitting) | S4.5.1 | Adequate |\n| exp-015 (SCS proxy) | S4.4 | Adequate |\n| exp-008-v2 (SDGD v2) | S4.5.1 | Adequate - two merge strategies |\n\n**Notable gaps:**\n- No error taxonomy analysis binned by BCS (exp-007 from the actual project). This is a significant omission -- the project records show strong mechanistic evidence (rho=1.0 between BCS bin and intra-batch substitution rate, chi-squared=6952). Without this, the paper lacks direct mechanistic evidence for HOW confusability causes errors, weakening the multi-channel claim.\n- No FewRel results. The project records show strong BCS effects on FewRel (-9.1pp at 16-rel, -31.2pp at 80-rel), which would strengthen the confusability claim even in the negative framing. The switch to DocRED (where confusability is weaker) without reporting FewRel is curious.\n- No embedding ablation table (2x3 matrix). The project records contain a rich cross-embedding analysis that would add depth.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles an important question with commendable experimental rigor, and negative results papers have value. However, several weaknesses prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) The strongest negative claim -- that merge amplification is \"structural\" and \"irreducible\" -- is insufficiently supported. Only simple merge strategies are tested, and the \"perfect oracle\" analysis is an extrapolation, not a formal result.\n\n(2) The LLaMA null result, while interesting, conflates model scale with model family. A single confound (32B vs 70B) prevents the strong \"model-specific\" conclusion.\n\n(3) The rhetorical asymmetry in interpreting r=0.156 (significant evidence for confusability) vs rho=0.18 (explains only 3% of variance for proxy quality) undermines the paper's internal consistency.\n\n(4) Key mechanistic evidence available in the project (error taxonomy by BCS, FewRel BCS effects) is omitted, weakening the story.\n\n(5) The factorial BCS effect is borderline non-significant (p=0.054), and no multiple-comparison correction is applied across the many statistical tests.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (a) including the error taxonomy analysis, (b) testing at least one more model at comparable scale, (c) formal analysis of the merge oracle upper bound rather than extrapolation, and (d) sensitivity analysis on the graph coloring threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a934cb3d93abad099 (use SendMessage with to: 'a934cb3d93abad099' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85090\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 156759</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e","uuid":"89e0cb84-6c2d-4285-9794-61415aaa1f39","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:54:36.669Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper: \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies schema complexity degradation in training-free LLM IE. It hypothesizes two degradation channels: (1) type count → recall degradation, (2) type confusability → type confusion. It develops SDGD (Schema DAG-Guided Decomposition) with variants (zero, cal, v2) and tests across 3 IE tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), 3 embeddings (Qwen3, LLaMA, MiniLM).\n\n### Key findings (all negative):\n1. Confusability predicts Qwen3 errors (r=0.156) but not LLaMA's (r=0.018) → model-specific, not universal\n2. SDGD-zero consistently worse than random batching (Δ=-0.061 F1, p=0.003 over 5 seeds)\n3. SDGD-cal marginal improvement over zero but still below single-pass (-16.7 F1) and random (-4.2)\n4. SDGD-v2 improved merge reduces conflicts 28%→19% but still -14.7 F1 vs single-pass\n5. Per-type splitting: highest recall (0.586) but lowest precision (0.194), 42% merge conflicts\n6. 2×2 factorial: count and BCS main effects directionally correct but interaction non-significant (F=0.42, p=0.52)\n7. DocRED: count channel generalizes (-44pp) but confusability channel nearly vanishes (4-5pp BCS delta)\n8. SCS proxy validation: best ρ=0.18, explains ~3% variance, top-50 recall only 0.28\n\n### Key tables:\n- Table 1: 2×2 factorial (Low/High Count × Low/High BCS) F1 values\n- Table 2: ANOVA (Count p=0.013, BCS p=0.054, interaction p=0.52)\n- Table 3: Cross-model pairwise SCS-confusion correlation\n- Table 4: Cross-model comparison of confusability effects\n- Table 5: LLaMA count curve (non-monotonic F1)\n- Table 6: SCS vs empirical confusion (ρ=0.05-0.18)\n- Table 7: Full decomposition comparison on Few-NERD (7 modes)\n- Table 8: 5-seed SDGD-zero vs random\n- Table 9: MAVEN decomposition results\n- Table 10: DocRED count curve\n- Table 11: DocRED BCS-controlled accuracy\n\n### Experiment coverage reference\nThe full experimental design (from review_context.yaml) includes these imagined experiments:\n1. DocRED Confusability Study (exp-005) — covered in paper §4.6\n2. Count × Confusability Interaction (exp-006) — covered in §4.2\n3. SDGD-zero on MAVEN (exp-009) — covered in §4.5.2\n4. SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (exp-010) — covered in §4.5.1\n5. SDGD-cal on MAVEN (exp-011) — covered in §4.5.2\n6. LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation (exp-012) — covered in §4.3\n7. Random Batching Ablation (exp-013) — covered in §4.5.1\n8. Per-Type Splitting (exp-014) — covered in §4.5.1\n9. SCS Proxy Validation (exp-015) — covered in §4.4\n10. SDGD v2 on Few-NERD (exp-008-v2) — covered in §4.5.1\n\n## Your review task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on experimental design, methodology rigor, statistical validity)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodological gaps, experimental limitations, statistical concerns, missing controls)\n4. **Detailed questions/comments** on specific experimental choices, statistical methodology, and results interpretation\n5. **Experiment coverage assessment**: Are the imagined experiments adequately covered? Any notable gaps?\n6. **Score** (1-6) with **confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Pay attention to:\n- Statistical rigor (effect sizes, significance tests, multiple comparisons)\n- Experimental controls and confounds\n- Whether the conclusions are supported by the evidence\n- Adequacy of baselines\n- Reproducibility concerns\n- Whether the \"negative results\" framing is justified or could mask methodological issues\n","agentId":"a934cb3d93abad099","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a complete picture. Let me now compose my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments Focus)**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas, hypothesizing two independent channels (type count causing recall loss, type confusability causing type confusion). The authors develop SDGD, a schema decomposition framework based on graph coloring over pairwise type similarity, tested across three IE tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), two LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), and three embedding spaces. The findings are uniformly negative: confusability is model-specific (significant for Qwen3 but null for LLaMA), all SDGD variants underperform single-pass baselines, and embedding-based confusability proxies are too weak (rho <= 0.18) to guide decomposition.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Rigorous controlled experimental design.** The 2x2 factorial (count x BCS), the systematic variation across 3 tasks, 2 models, and 3 embeddings, and the multi-level analysis (pairwise, per-type, batch) demonstrate commendable methodological discipline. The controlled batch construction with fixed count and varying BCS is a clean experimental design for isolating confusability.\n\n- **Appropriate statistical methodology for the factorial analysis.** The ANOVA with effect sizes (eta-squared), paired t-tests with 5 seeds for SDGD-zero vs. random, and reporting of both Pearson and Spearman correlations are sound. The significance levels are properly reported.\n\n- **Transparent reporting of negative results.** The paper does not bury the failures. The 5-seed SDGD-zero vs. random comparison (Table 8) with 100% consistency and p=0.003 is a well-designed ablation. The full decomposition spectrum (Table 7) from single-pass through per-type splitting provides comprehensive coverage.\n\n- **Mechanistic diagnosis.** The merge amplification analysis -- showing that precision loss has a structural component beyond merge conflicts (estimated ~0.35 F1 even with perfect merge oracle) -- is a useful insight for the field.\n\n- **Multi-model validation is attempted honestly.** Rather than cherry-picking the positive Qwen3 result, the authors confront the LLaMA null head-on, testing all three embeddings to rule out embedding choice as an explanation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"negative result\" framing may mask methodological deficiencies in the merge strategy.** The paper's core claim that merge amplification is \"structural\" and \"irreducible\" rests on testing only naive union merge, confidence-weighted dedup, and type-priority resolution. These are three relatively simple merge strategies. The claim that \"even a hypothetical perfect merge oracle would yield ~0.35 F1\" is stated but not formally derived -- it appears to be an extrapolation from the observation that merge conflicts account for ~40% of precision loss. More sophisticated approaches (e.g., LLM-based re-ranking of conflicting predictions, iterative multi-pass refinement, or constrained decoding) are acknowledged in Limitations but dismissed without testing. Calling the bottleneck \"fundamental\" after testing three simple strategies overstates the evidence.\n\n- **Confound between batch size and merge difficulty.** SDGD uses k=8 batches for 66 types (average ~8 types/batch), while random batching uses the same k. But SDGD's graph coloring may produce highly unbalanced batches (the project records show batches of [18,12,11,7,7,5,3,3] before v2 min_batch_size enforcement). Small batches (3 types) over-predict 4.4x while large batches (18 types) under-predict 0.6x. The paper does not adequately control for batch size distribution -- SDGD-zero's inferior performance may be driven by its unbalanced batch sizes rather than by confusability-aware grouping being harmful per se.\n\n- **The LLaMA null result has alternative explanations that are under-explored.** The paper attributes LLaMA's null confusability signal to a fundamentally different error profile (omission vs. substitution). But LLaMA-3.3-70B is a 70B model vs. Qwen3's 32B -- the model size difference alone could explain better type discrimination. The claim that this reveals \"model-specificity of confusability\" would be much stronger if tested on another 32B model (e.g., LLaMA-3.3-32B or a similar-scale model). As is, the paper cannot distinguish \"model family effect\" from \"model scale effect.\"\n\n- **The SCS proxy validation (Table 6) numbers are suspiciously low compared to the pairwise correlation results.** The paper reports pairwise SCS-confusion r=0.156 (p<1e-11) for Qwen3 on Few-NERD but SCS proxy rho=0.18. If SCS and confusion have r=0.156 in Pearson terms, the Spearman rho should be comparable or higher (Spearman is more robust to outliers). The jump from r=0.156 (Table 3) to rho=0.18 (Table 6) is small but plausible; however, the framing is inconsistent -- the pairwise correlation is presented as \"significant evidence for confusability\" while the nearly identical number is presented as \"explains only ~3% of variance\" when evaluating the proxy. This rhetorical asymmetry undermines trust.\n\n- **BCS effect in the factorial is borderline and inconsistently interpreted.** BCS main effect F=3.90, p=0.054 (Table 2) is not significant at alpha=0.05. The paper acknowledges this but still treats confusability as a validated channel. In a negative-results paper, this borderline result deserves more careful treatment -- is confusability even a real signal on Qwen3 at the batch level, or only at the pairwise level?\n\n- **No correction for multiple comparisons.** The paper tests pairwise correlations across 3 embeddings x 2 models x 2+ datasets, plus per-type correlations, batch-level effects, and factorial effects. No multiple-comparison correction (Bonferroni, FDR) is applied. Given the number of tests, some \"significant\" results may be false positives. This is particularly concerning for the LLaMA analysis where 6+ tests are run and all are null -- the absence of correction cuts both ways.\n\n- **DocRED pairwise correlation (r=0.038, p=0.082) is borderline non-significant but treated as definitively null.** With n=4,560 pairs, even small effects should be detectable. The paper interprets this as \"confusability channel nearly vanishes\" on document-level RE, but the confidence interval on r likely includes values up to ~0.06-0.07. A more measured conclusion would note that the effect may exist but is smaller than on sentence-level tasks.\n\n- **Limited evaluation metrics.** The paper uses F1/precision/recall for NER/ED and accuracy for RE. No evaluation of entity detection (span-level) vs. entity classification (type-level) separately, which would directly test the multi-channel model. The count channel should manifest in span detection; the confusability channel should manifest in type classification given correct spans. This decomposed evaluation is crucial for the two-channel claim but absent.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Detailed Questions and Comments\n\n**Q1 (Batch construction methodology):** How are \"low BCS\" and \"high BCS\" batches constructed? If by extremal selection (top/bottom quartile of possible batches), the effect sizes are inflated relative to what SDGD would encounter on average. The paper should report the BCS distribution of random batches and where the selected batches fall.\n\n**Q2 (SDGD-zero batch composition):** The project records reveal that SDGD-zero produced batches of [18,12,11,7,7,5,3,3] types, with 3-type batches over-predicting 4.4x. Was the v2 min_batch_size=8 constraint applied before the results in Table 7? If not, the SDGD-zero failure is substantially attributable to degenerate batch sizes rather than confusability-aware grouping being harmful. This distinction is critical for the paper's central negative claim.\n\n**Q3 (ANOVA cell sizes):** How many batches per cell in the 2x2 factorial? If each cell has only 5 random batches (as suggested by the DocRED count curve design), the ANOVA has very low power. The non-significant interaction (F=0.42, p=0.52) cannot distinguish \"no interaction\" from \"insufficient power to detect interaction.\"\n\n**Q4 (LLaMA non-monotonic F1):** The LLaMA count curve shows F1 rising from 0.275 (count=22) to 0.395 (count=44). This is a striking non-monotonicity that is explained as \"precision-recall tradeoff\" but deserves deeper investigation. Does the model switch extraction strategies at different prompt lengths? Are there ceiling effects in the prompt format?\n\n**Q5 (Graph coloring threshold):** The threshold tau=0.8 is mentioned but no sensitivity analysis is presented. Was this chosen to optimize SDGD's performance or fixed a priori? If optimized, the negative results are stronger; if fixed a priori, SDGD may have failed due to a poor threshold choice.\n\n**Q6 (Calibration data for SDGD-cal):** With only 5 examples per type and 66 types, most type pairs will have zero co-occurrences in the calibration set. How is the empirical confusion matrix computed for pairs with no calibration co-occurrence? If assumed to be zero, the matrix is extremely sparse and the claim that \"empirical calibration cannot overcome merge amplification\" is confounded by insufficient calibration data.\n\n**Q7 (Sample size for evaluation):** How many test instances are used for each evaluation? The paper does not report dataset splits or test set sizes. With small test sets, the F1 differences could be within sampling noise.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 10 imagined experiments from the review context are covered in the paper:\n\n| Experiment | Section | Coverage |\n|---|---|---|\n| exp-005 (DocRED) | S4.6 | Adequate - count curve and BCS-controlled results reported |\n| exp-006 (2x2 Factorial) | S4.2 | Adequate - ANOVA with effect sizes |\n| exp-009 (SDGD-zero MAVEN) | S4.5.2 | Adequate |\n| exp-010 (SDGD-cal Few-NERD) | S4.5.1 | Adequate |\n| exp-011 (SDGD-cal MAVEN) | S4.5.2 | Adequate |\n| exp-012 (LLaMA validation) | S4.3 | **Strong** - comprehensive multi-embedding, multi-level |\n| exp-013 (Random ablation) | S4.5.1 | Adequate - 5-seed comparison |\n| exp-014 (Per-type splitting) | S4.5.1 | Adequate |\n| exp-015 (SCS proxy) | S4.4 | Adequate |\n| exp-008-v2 (SDGD v2) | S4.5.1 | Adequate - two merge strategies |\n\n**Notable gaps:**\n- No error taxonomy analysis binned by BCS (exp-007 from the actual project). This is a significant omission -- the project records show strong mechanistic evidence (rho=1.0 between BCS bin and intra-batch substitution rate, chi-squared=6952). Without this, the paper lacks direct mechanistic evidence for HOW confusability causes errors, weakening the multi-channel claim.\n- No FewRel results. The project records show strong BCS effects on FewRel (-9.1pp at 16-rel, -31.2pp at 80-rel), which would strengthen the confusability claim even in the negative framing. The switch to DocRED (where confusability is weaker) without reporting FewRel is curious.\n- No embedding ablation table (2x3 matrix). The project records contain a rich cross-embedding analysis that would add depth.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper tackles an important question with commendable experimental rigor, and negative results papers have value. However, several weaknesses prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) The strongest negative claim -- that merge amplification is \"structural\" and \"irreducible\" -- is insufficiently supported. Only simple merge strategies are tested, and the \"perfect oracle\" analysis is an extrapolation, not a formal result.\n\n(2) The LLaMA null result, while interesting, conflates model scale with model family. A single confound (32B vs 70B) prevents the strong \"model-specific\" conclusion.\n\n(3) The rhetorical asymmetry in interpreting r=0.156 (significant evidence for confusability) vs rho=0.18 (explains only 3% of variance for proxy quality) undermines the paper's internal consistency.\n\n(4) Key mechanistic evidence available in the project (error taxonomy by BCS, FewRel BCS effects) is omitted, weakening the story.\n\n(5) The factorial BCS effect is borderline non-significant (p=0.054), and no multiple-comparison correction is applied across the many statistical tests.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (a) including the error taxonomy analysis, (b) testing at least one more model at comparable scale, (c) formal analysis of the merge oracle upper bound rather than extrapolation, and (d) sensitivity analysis on the graph coloring threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":156759,"totalTokens":85090,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13815,"cache_read_input_tokens":67914,"output_tokens":3360,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13815},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3360,"cache_read_input_tokens":67914,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13815,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13815,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
