{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SKFYhuRUbBXtLSeGa24Z8G","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental record, related work landscape, and prior dream reviews. The prompt asks me to review the paper as described in the summary -- the \"negative results\" framing where SDGD fails. This corresponds to the median-tier paper. Let me compose my R2 review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas, proposing a two-channel degradation model (type count drives recall loss; type confusability drives type confusion). The authors develop SDGD, a graph-coloring-based schema decomposition framework grounded in embedding-based confusability scores. Across three IE tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), two LLMs, and three embedding models, SDGD consistently fails to outperform single-pass extraction (delta = -0.061 F1, p=0.003), leading the authors to identify \"merge amplification\" as a fundamental bottleneck. The paper is framed as negative results that reframe assumptions about schema decomposition in LLM IE.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **S1. Genuinely useful diagnostic contribution.** The multi-channel degradation model -- disentangling count-driven recall loss from confusability-driven type confusion -- is a real conceptual contribution. The 2x2 factorial design testing channel orthogonality is methodologically sound and the kind of controlled experiment rarely seen in LLM IE work.\n\n- **S2. The merge amplification finding is novel and actionable.** Identifying that decomposition methods are bottlenecked by the merge/reassembly step rather than the partition step is a genuinely surprising and practically important insight. The 42% merge conflict rate and the observation that even per-type splitting (which should eliminate within-batch confusion) suffers precision collapse is informative for future work in this area.\n\n- **S3. Cross-task breadth.** Testing across NER (66 types), ED (168 types), and RE (80-96 types) with genuinely different schema structures (cross-domain vs. same-domain) goes beyond the typical single-task evaluation in LLM IE papers.\n\n- **S4. The centrality confound analysis is a methodological contribution.** The finding that per-type aggregation of confusability (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound on homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), while pairwise confusability remains valid, is useful guidance for future work on schema analysis.\n\n- **S5. Honest reporting of negative results.** The paper does not hide that SDGD fails. The progression from SDGD-zero to SDGD-cal to SDGD-v2, each failing to close the gap, is transparently presented. This kind of systematic negative result is valuable when the direction being ruled out is one that others might otherwise pursue.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 (Major). The related work section is drastically inadequate.** Six references for a paper targeting EMNLP Main is far below the expected standard. The paper misses large swaths of directly relevant literature:\n\n  - **Schema decomposition methods:** LLM-PEE (Zhang et al. 2024/2025), which already performs embedding-similarity-based type partitioning for LLM event extraction, is the single most important missing reference. It is essentially the same algorithmic idea as SDGD-zero (partition types by similarity using embeddings) and must be cited and compared. PARSE (Shrimal et al. 2025, EMNLP Industry) performs LLM-driven schema rewriting. DEE (2024) decomposes event extraction with schema-aware RAG. SPT (2025) frames schemas as parameterized tools. DLISC (2025) uses dual-LoRA with schema caching. None are cited.\n\n  - **Multi-agent approaches to type confusion:** CROSSAGENTIE (Lu et al. 2025, ACL Findings) directly addresses type confusion through cross-type debate -- a reactive strategy complementary to SDGD's proactive decomposition. CMAS-NER (2025, TheWebConf) uses cooperative multi-agent frameworks for zero-shot NER. Both are directly relevant and uncited.\n\n  - **Zero-shot IE baselines:** GoLLIE (Sainz et al. 2024, ICLR), GLiNER/GLiNER2 (Zaratiana et al. 2024-2025, NAACL/EMNLP Demo), Code4UIE (Guo et al. 2024, NeurIPS Workshop), IEPile (Chen et al. 2024, ACL), KnowCoder (2024, ACL), Self-Improving NER (2024, NAACL), ZeroNER (2025, ACL Findings), ReasoningNER (2025) -- none are cited. The paper gives the impression that LLM IE consists of GPT-NER, UniNER, and nothing else.\n\n  - **Embedding anisotropy literature:** The centrality confound finding connects directly to work on representation degeneration (Gao et al. 2019, ICLR), contextual embedding anisotropy (Ethayarajh 2019, EMNLP), isotropy clusters (Cai et al. 2021, ICLR), and inherent self-attention anisotropy (Godey et al. 2024, EACL). These provide the theoretical explanation for why embedding-based SCS fails on homogeneous schemas. None are cited.\n\n  - **Surveys:** Xu et al. (2024) provide a comprehensive survey of LLM-based generative IE. Uncited.\n\n  A paper claiming to \"reframe assumptions\" about schema decomposition for LLM IE cannot do so while citing only 6 papers. The related work section reads as if it was written without a literature review.\n\n- **W2 (Major). Novelty of the negative findings is questionable.** The three negative findings need scrutiny:\n\n  (a) \"Confusability is model-specific\" (Qwen3 r=0.156 vs LLaMA r=0.018): That different models exhibit different confusion patterns is expected -- different architectures, different training data, different internal representations. This is not a surprising negative result; it is a predictable observation. The framing overstates its significance.\n\n  (b) \"SDGD underperforms single-pass\": The paper essentially shows that splitting extraction into batches and merging results loses information and introduces errors. This is a well-known problem in any decompose-and-merge pipeline -- it is the fundamental challenge of ensemble methods, committee machines, and multi-expert systems. The specific instantiation in IE is somewhat new, but the general principle that naive decomposition + union merge degrades precision is not novel.\n\n  (c) \"Merge amplification is structural\": This is the most interesting finding, but the paper does not adequately distinguish it from the well-known precision-recall tradeoff in ensemble/voting systems. The 42% merge conflict rate is a useful quantification, but the underlying phenomenon (independent classifiers disagree on ambiguous instances) has been studied for decades.\n\n- **W3 (Major). The \"negative results\" framing may mask insufficient method development.** The paper proposes SDGD-zero, finds it fails catastrophically (F1=0.251 vs 0.445 single-pass), diagnoses merge amplification as the root cause, then tries SDGD-v2 with confidence-gated merge, which still leaves a -14.7 F1 gap. A reviewer could reasonably ask: why not develop the merge strategy further? Confidence thresholding is a minimal intervention. More sophisticated approaches -- span-level voting with learned weights, cross-batch consistency decoding, hierarchical refinement -- are mentioned in discussion but not attempted. The paper essentially tries one obvious approach, finds it does not work, tries one obvious fix, finds it partially works but not enough, and declares the direction dead. This feels premature for a main conference paper.\n\n- **W4 (Major). Small effect sizes undermine the confusability contribution.** Pairwise r=0.156 (Few-NERD) and r=0.120 (MAVEN) explain 1.4-2.4% of confusion variance. For LLaMA, r=0.018 is essentially zero. The paper leans on statistical significance inflated by large sample sizes (1,953 and 11,026 pairs). A statistically significant but practically negligible correlation does not constitute strong evidence for confusability as a meaningful degradation channel. The error taxonomy (49.4% intra-batch substitution at BCS >= 0.95) is more convincing, but the gap between the weak pairwise correlation and the strong error-taxonomy result is not adequately explained.\n\n- **W5 (Minor). Contribution significance for a top venue.** \"We tried decomposition, it didn't work\" is a valid negative result only if the decomposition approach was well-motivated, rigorously developed, and the negative finding is genuinely surprising. Given the minimal method development (threshold-based graph coloring + union merge, then confidence-gated merge), a reviewer may see this as \"obvious approach fails for obvious reasons.\" For EMNLP Main (25% acceptance rate), the analytical contributions (multi-channel model, centrality confound) are more publication-worthy than the negative SDGD result, but the paper frames SDGD failure as the headline.\n\n- **W6 (Minor). Positioning within LLM IE literature is incomplete.** The paper positions itself against GPT-NER and UniNER but does not engage with the broader landscape of structured prediction with LLMs, prompt engineering for complex schemas, or the growing multi-agent IE literature. This makes it difficult to assess whether the findings truly \"reframe\" assumptions or merely confirm what practitioners already suspect.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Detailed Comments\n\n**Novelty assessment.** The paper contains three layers of novelty: (1) the multi-channel degradation model (count vs. confusability), (2) the pairwise confusability analysis with embedding ablation, and (3) the negative SDGD result with merge amplification diagnosis.\n\nLayer (1) is the strongest novelty claim. While the individual phenomena (more classes hurt recall; similar classes cause confusion) are well-known in classification, the controlled factorial demonstration in training-free LLM IE with orthogonality testing is new. However, the \"multi-channel degradation model\" label implies more theoretical depth than exists -- these are two empirically observed trends, not a formal model derived from first principles.\n\nLayer (2) is moderate novelty. IEPile (ACL 2024) already constructs hard-negative schemas (semantically proximate type sets) during training, confirming that confusable schemas are harder. The Familiarity metric (NAACL 2025) quantifies label similarity effects on evaluation. The paper's contribution is moving this to inference-time analysis in training-free settings, which is a valid but incremental distinction.\n\nLayer (3) -- the negative SDGD result -- is the weakest novelty claim. The merge amplification finding is interesting but undersold: the paper diagnoses it well but does not develop it into an actionable contribution (e.g., a better merge algorithm, theoretical analysis of when decomposition can work, tight bounds on merge error).\n\n**Related work completeness.** Severely lacking. See W1 above. The paper needs at minimum 20-25 references to adequately cover: (a) schema decomposition methods (LLM-PEE, PARSE, DEE, SPT, DLISC), (b) multi-agent IE (CROSSAGENTIE, CMAS-NER), (c) zero-shot IE baselines (GoLLIE, GLiNER, IEPile, KnowCoder, ZeroNER), (d) embedding geometry (Ethayarajh 2019, Cai et al. 2021, Godey et al. 2024), (e) negative results methodology (beyond Rogers et al. 2020), and (f) at least one comprehensive IE survey. Six references is unacceptable for a venue-track paper.\n\n**Contribution significance.** The analytical contributions (Claims 1-2: multi-channel degradation, pairwise confusability signal, centrality confound) are solid Findings-level material. The negative SDGD result alone is insufficient for Main -- it needs either (a) a much more thorough development of decomposition approaches before declaring the direction limited, or (b) a strong theoretical analysis explaining why decomposition must fail under certain conditions, or (c) a positive prescriptive contribution that emerges from the negative finding (e.g., a merge algorithm that actually works).\n\n**Framing.** \"Negative results\" is a legitimate framing if the result is surprising and the attempted approach was well-developed. Here, the approach (threshold graph coloring + union merge) is minimal, and the failure mode (merge errors) is somewhat predictable. A stronger framing would be: \"We present a diagnostic framework for schema complexity in LLM IE, identify merge amplification as the binding constraint on decomposition, and provide actionable guidelines for when decomposition helps vs. hurts.\" This shifts the emphasis from \"it didn't work\" to \"here is why and when it can't work,\" which is a more substantial contribution.\n\n**Positioning within broader LLM IE.** The paper exists in a rapidly growing space where multiple groups are working on schema-aware LLM IE from different angles (tool-based, multi-agent, code-style, retrieval-augmented). Without engaging with this literature, the paper's contributions appear isolated rather than situated within a coherent research program. The omission of LLM-PEE is particularly damaging since it is the most direct comparison point.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Justification:** The analytical contributions (multi-channel degradation model, pairwise confusability signal, centrality confound, merge amplification diagnosis) are genuine and useful. However, three factors prevent acceptance: (1) the related work section with only 6 references is far below venue standards and misses the most important comparison point (LLM-PEE); (2) the negative SDGD result follows minimal method development, making the finding feel more like \"first attempt didn't work\" than \"systematic ruling out of a promising direction\"; (3) the pairwise effect sizes are small (1.4-2.4% variance explained) and model-dependent (near-zero for LLaMA), weakening the central confusability claim. With a comprehensive related work section, head-to-head LLM-PEE comparison, and either deeper method development or theoretical analysis of decomposition limits, this could be a solid Findings paper. As submitted, it falls below the Main conference threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1f46fe4235d5120f (use SendMessage with to: 'a1f46fe4235d5120f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 76154\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 151868</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e","uuid":"77f0a13a-228c-49a1-9aba-74c97857cdd9","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:54:50.351Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper: \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper studies schema complexity degradation in training-free LLM IE. It hypothesizes two degradation channels: type count (→recall) and type confusability (→type confusion). It develops SDGD (Schema DAG-Guided Decomposition) using graph coloring based on embedding similarity, with variants: SDGD-zero (embedding SCS), SDGD-cal (empirical calibration), SDGD-v2 (improved merge). Tests on 3 tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B), 3 embeddings.\n\n### Three main negative findings:\n1. Confusability is model-specific (Qwen3 r=0.156 vs LLaMA r=0.018), not a universal schema property\n2. SDGD consistently underperforms single-pass and even random batching (Δ=-0.061 F1, p=0.003)\n3. Merge amplification is structural: even improved merge (SDGD-v2) leaves -14.7 F1 gap; per-type splitting shows 42% merge conflicts\n\n### Related work section (§2, brief):\n- Training-free LLM IE: GPT-NER (Wang et al. 2023), Li et al. 2023, UniNER (Zhou et al. 2024), LLM-PEE\n- Schema decomposition: \"Prior methods operate on unvalidated assumptions\"\n- Negative results in NLP: Rogers et al. 2020, \"Lost in the Middle\" (Liu et al. 2024)\n\n### Paper structure:\n- 9 pages, 11 tables, claims to release code/data\n- Positions as \"negative results that reframe assumptions\"\n- Targets EMNLP Main / Findings\n\n### References (only 6 total):\n1. Wang et al. 2023 - GPT-NER\n2. Li et al. 2023 - ChatGPT IE evaluation\n3. Lu et al. 2023 - Chameleon\n4. Zhou et al. 2024 - UniNER\n5. Rogers et al. 2020 - BERTology primer\n6. Liu et al. 2024 - Lost in the Middle\n\n## Your review task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty of contributions, positioning, significance for the field)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty concerns, related work gaps, positioning issues, contribution significance)\n4. **Detailed comments** on:\n   - Novelty assessment: Are the negative findings genuinely surprising or somewhat expected?\n   - Related work completeness: What important prior work is missing?\n   - Contribution significance: Do the negative results constitute sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n   - Framing: Is \"negative results\" the right frame, or does it mask insufficient method development?\n   - Positioning within the broader LLM IE literature\n5. **Score** (1-6) with **confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Whether the related work section adequately covers the landscape (schema decomposition methods, LLM-based IE beyond GPT-NER, structured prediction with LLMs, prompt engineering for complex schemas, etc.)\n- Whether the novelty claims are overstated or understated\n- Whether the \"methodological contribution\" framing (multi-channel degradation, embedding ablation, merge amplification) is substantive\n- Whether a reviewer would see this as \"they tried something obvious, it didn't work\" vs \"they systematically ruled out a promising direction\"\n- The paper has only 6 references — is this adequate?\n","agentId":"a1f46fe4235d5120f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental record, related work landscape, and prior dream reviews. The prompt asks me to review the paper as described in the summary -- the \"negative results\" framing where SDGD fails. This corresponds to the median-tier paper. Let me compose my R2 review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"One Size Does Not Fit All: Negative Results on Schema Decomposition for Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why training-free LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas, proposing a two-channel degradation model (type count drives recall loss; type confusability drives type confusion). The authors develop SDGD, a graph-coloring-based schema decomposition framework grounded in embedding-based confusability scores. Across three IE tasks (NER/Few-NERD, ED/MAVEN, RE/DocRED), two LLMs, and three embedding models, SDGD consistently fails to outperform single-pass extraction (delta = -0.061 F1, p=0.003), leading the authors to identify \"merge amplification\" as a fundamental bottleneck. The paper is framed as negative results that reframe assumptions about schema decomposition in LLM IE.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **S1. Genuinely useful diagnostic contribution.** The multi-channel degradation model -- disentangling count-driven recall loss from confusability-driven type confusion -- is a real conceptual contribution. The 2x2 factorial design testing channel orthogonality is methodologically sound and the kind of controlled experiment rarely seen in LLM IE work.\n\n- **S2. The merge amplification finding is novel and actionable.** Identifying that decomposition methods are bottlenecked by the merge/reassembly step rather than the partition step is a genuinely surprising and practically important insight. The 42% merge conflict rate and the observation that even per-type splitting (which should eliminate within-batch confusion) suffers precision collapse is informative for future work in this area.\n\n- **S3. Cross-task breadth.** Testing across NER (66 types), ED (168 types), and RE (80-96 types) with genuinely different schema structures (cross-domain vs. same-domain) goes beyond the typical single-task evaluation in LLM IE papers.\n\n- **S4. The centrality confound analysis is a methodological contribution.** The finding that per-type aggregation of confusability (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound on homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), while pairwise confusability remains valid, is useful guidance for future work on schema analysis.\n\n- **S5. Honest reporting of negative results.** The paper does not hide that SDGD fails. The progression from SDGD-zero to SDGD-cal to SDGD-v2, each failing to close the gap, is transparently presented. This kind of systematic negative result is valuable when the direction being ruled out is one that others might otherwise pursue.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 (Major). The related work section is drastically inadequate.** Six references for a paper targeting EMNLP Main is far below the expected standard. The paper misses large swaths of directly relevant literature:\n\n  - **Schema decomposition methods:** LLM-PEE (Zhang et al. 2024/2025), which already performs embedding-similarity-based type partitioning for LLM event extraction, is the single most important missing reference. It is essentially the same algorithmic idea as SDGD-zero (partition types by similarity using embeddings) and must be cited and compared. PARSE (Shrimal et al. 2025, EMNLP Industry) performs LLM-driven schema rewriting. DEE (2024) decomposes event extraction with schema-aware RAG. SPT (2025) frames schemas as parameterized tools. DLISC (2025) uses dual-LoRA with schema caching. None are cited.\n\n  - **Multi-agent approaches to type confusion:** CROSSAGENTIE (Lu et al. 2025, ACL Findings) directly addresses type confusion through cross-type debate -- a reactive strategy complementary to SDGD's proactive decomposition. CMAS-NER (2025, TheWebConf) uses cooperative multi-agent frameworks for zero-shot NER. Both are directly relevant and uncited.\n\n  - **Zero-shot IE baselines:** GoLLIE (Sainz et al. 2024, ICLR), GLiNER/GLiNER2 (Zaratiana et al. 2024-2025, NAACL/EMNLP Demo), Code4UIE (Guo et al. 2024, NeurIPS Workshop), IEPile (Chen et al. 2024, ACL), KnowCoder (2024, ACL), Self-Improving NER (2024, NAACL), ZeroNER (2025, ACL Findings), ReasoningNER (2025) -- none are cited. The paper gives the impression that LLM IE consists of GPT-NER, UniNER, and nothing else.\n\n  - **Embedding anisotropy literature:** The centrality confound finding connects directly to work on representation degeneration (Gao et al. 2019, ICLR), contextual embedding anisotropy (Ethayarajh 2019, EMNLP), isotropy clusters (Cai et al. 2021, ICLR), and inherent self-attention anisotropy (Godey et al. 2024, EACL). These provide the theoretical explanation for why embedding-based SCS fails on homogeneous schemas. None are cited.\n\n  - **Surveys:** Xu et al. (2024) provide a comprehensive survey of LLM-based generative IE. Uncited.\n\n  A paper claiming to \"reframe assumptions\" about schema decomposition for LLM IE cannot do so while citing only 6 papers. The related work section reads as if it was written without a literature review.\n\n- **W2 (Major). Novelty of the negative findings is questionable.** The three negative findings need scrutiny:\n\n  (a) \"Confusability is model-specific\" (Qwen3 r=0.156 vs LLaMA r=0.018): That different models exhibit different confusion patterns is expected -- different architectures, different training data, different internal representations. This is not a surprising negative result; it is a predictable observation. The framing overstates its significance.\n\n  (b) \"SDGD underperforms single-pass\": The paper essentially shows that splitting extraction into batches and merging results loses information and introduces errors. This is a well-known problem in any decompose-and-merge pipeline -- it is the fundamental challenge of ensemble methods, committee machines, and multi-expert systems. The specific instantiation in IE is somewhat new, but the general principle that naive decomposition + union merge degrades precision is not novel.\n\n  (c) \"Merge amplification is structural\": This is the most interesting finding, but the paper does not adequately distinguish it from the well-known precision-recall tradeoff in ensemble/voting systems. The 42% merge conflict rate is a useful quantification, but the underlying phenomenon (independent classifiers disagree on ambiguous instances) has been studied for decades.\n\n- **W3 (Major). The \"negative results\" framing may mask insufficient method development.** The paper proposes SDGD-zero, finds it fails catastrophically (F1=0.251 vs 0.445 single-pass), diagnoses merge amplification as the root cause, then tries SDGD-v2 with confidence-gated merge, which still leaves a -14.7 F1 gap. A reviewer could reasonably ask: why not develop the merge strategy further? Confidence thresholding is a minimal intervention. More sophisticated approaches -- span-level voting with learned weights, cross-batch consistency decoding, hierarchical refinement -- are mentioned in discussion but not attempted. The paper essentially tries one obvious approach, finds it does not work, tries one obvious fix, finds it partially works but not enough, and declares the direction dead. This feels premature for a main conference paper.\n\n- **W4 (Major). Small effect sizes undermine the confusability contribution.** Pairwise r=0.156 (Few-NERD) and r=0.120 (MAVEN) explain 1.4-2.4% of confusion variance. For LLaMA, r=0.018 is essentially zero. The paper leans on statistical significance inflated by large sample sizes (1,953 and 11,026 pairs). A statistically significant but practically negligible correlation does not constitute strong evidence for confusability as a meaningful degradation channel. The error taxonomy (49.4% intra-batch substitution at BCS >= 0.95) is more convincing, but the gap between the weak pairwise correlation and the strong error-taxonomy result is not adequately explained.\n\n- **W5 (Minor). Contribution significance for a top venue.** \"We tried decomposition, it didn't work\" is a valid negative result only if the decomposition approach was well-motivated, rigorously developed, and the negative finding is genuinely surprising. Given the minimal method development (threshold-based graph coloring + union merge, then confidence-gated merge), a reviewer may see this as \"obvious approach fails for obvious reasons.\" For EMNLP Main (25% acceptance rate), the analytical contributions (multi-channel model, centrality confound) are more publication-worthy than the negative SDGD result, but the paper frames SDGD failure as the headline.\n\n- **W6 (Minor). Positioning within LLM IE literature is incomplete.** The paper positions itself against GPT-NER and UniNER but does not engage with the broader landscape of structured prediction with LLMs, prompt engineering for complex schemas, or the growing multi-agent IE literature. This makes it difficult to assess whether the findings truly \"reframe\" assumptions or merely confirm what practitioners already suspect.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Detailed Comments\n\n**Novelty assessment.** The paper contains three layers of novelty: (1) the multi-channel degradation model (count vs. confusability), (2) the pairwise confusability analysis with embedding ablation, and (3) the negative SDGD result with merge amplification diagnosis.\n\nLayer (1) is the strongest novelty claim. While the individual phenomena (more classes hurt recall; similar classes cause confusion) are well-known in classification, the controlled factorial demonstration in training-free LLM IE with orthogonality testing is new. However, the \"multi-channel degradation model\" label implies more theoretical depth than exists -- these are two empirically observed trends, not a formal model derived from first principles.\n\nLayer (2) is moderate novelty. IEPile (ACL 2024) already constructs hard-negative schemas (semantically proximate type sets) during training, confirming that confusable schemas are harder. The Familiarity metric (NAACL 2025) quantifies label similarity effects on evaluation. The paper's contribution is moving this to inference-time analysis in training-free settings, which is a valid but incremental distinction.\n\nLayer (3) -- the negative SDGD result -- is the weakest novelty claim. The merge amplification finding is interesting but undersold: the paper diagnoses it well but does not develop it into an actionable contribution (e.g., a better merge algorithm, theoretical analysis of when decomposition can work, tight bounds on merge error).\n\n**Related work completeness.** Severely lacking. See W1 above. The paper needs at minimum 20-25 references to adequately cover: (a) schema decomposition methods (LLM-PEE, PARSE, DEE, SPT, DLISC), (b) multi-agent IE (CROSSAGENTIE, CMAS-NER), (c) zero-shot IE baselines (GoLLIE, GLiNER, IEPile, KnowCoder, ZeroNER), (d) embedding geometry (Ethayarajh 2019, Cai et al. 2021, Godey et al. 2024), (e) negative results methodology (beyond Rogers et al. 2020), and (f) at least one comprehensive IE survey. Six references is unacceptable for a venue-track paper.\n\n**Contribution significance.** The analytical contributions (Claims 1-2: multi-channel degradation, pairwise confusability signal, centrality confound) are solid Findings-level material. The negative SDGD result alone is insufficient for Main -- it needs either (a) a much more thorough development of decomposition approaches before declaring the direction limited, or (b) a strong theoretical analysis explaining why decomposition must fail under certain conditions, or (c) a positive prescriptive contribution that emerges from the negative finding (e.g., a merge algorithm that actually works).\n\n**Framing.** \"Negative results\" is a legitimate framing if the result is surprising and the attempted approach was well-developed. Here, the approach (threshold graph coloring + union merge) is minimal, and the failure mode (merge errors) is somewhat predictable. A stronger framing would be: \"We present a diagnostic framework for schema complexity in LLM IE, identify merge amplification as the binding constraint on decomposition, and provide actionable guidelines for when decomposition helps vs. hurts.\" This shifts the emphasis from \"it didn't work\" to \"here is why and when it can't work,\" which is a more substantial contribution.\n\n**Positioning within broader LLM IE.** The paper exists in a rapidly growing space where multiple groups are working on schema-aware LLM IE from different angles (tool-based, multi-agent, code-style, retrieval-augmented). Without engaging with this literature, the paper's contributions appear isolated rather than situated within a coherent research program. The omission of LLM-PEE is particularly damaging since it is the most direct comparison point.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Justification:** The analytical contributions (multi-channel degradation model, pairwise confusability signal, centrality confound, merge amplification diagnosis) are genuine and useful. However, three factors prevent acceptance: (1) the related work section with only 6 references is far below venue standards and misses the most important comparison point (LLM-PEE); (2) the negative SDGD result follows minimal method development, making the finding feel more like \"first attempt didn't work\" than \"systematic ruling out of a promising direction\"; (3) the pairwise effect sizes are small (1.4-2.4% variance explained) and model-dependent (near-zero for LLaMA), weakening the central confusability claim. With a comprehensive related work section, head-to-head LLM-PEE comparison, and either deeper method development or theoretical analysis of decomposition limits, this could be a solid Findings paper. As submitted, it falls below the Main conference threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":151868,"totalTokens":76154,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":721,"cache_read_input_tokens":71915,"output_tokens":3515,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":721},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3515,"cache_read_input_tokens":71915,"cache_creation_input_tokens":721,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":721,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01X76PCMTsdjQ3E9biKE52i2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable:\n\n**Step 1: Independent reviews** — Done (R1: 3/Weak Reject, R2: 3/Weak Reject)\n\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing** — I need to share each reviewer's findings with the other and get their reactions.\n\n**Step 3: Convergence** — Find areas of agreement/disagreement and resolve.\n\n**Step 4: Synthesis** — Produce the final consolidated review report.\n\nLet me now do Step 2 by sending cross-sharing prompts to both reviewers, then synthesize.\n\n\nActually, I should handle the cross-sharing and convergence myself as the director rather than cycling back to the agents. Let me synthesize the roundtable discussion directly and produce the final report. Both reviewers gave weak reject scores, so I'm looking at their key findings—R1 highlighted the rigorous experimental design with appropriate statistical methods and transparent reporting of negative results, while also noting a mechanistic diagnosis of merge amplification issues.\n\nHowever, R1 raised several methodological concerns: the structural merge amplification claim lacks sufficient support since only simple merge strategies were tested, there's a batch size confound with SDGD producing unbalanced batches, the LLaMA null result conflates model scale with model family differences, and there's rhetorical inconsistency in how correlation values are interpreted. The BCS effect is borderline significant at p=0.054, there's no multiple comparison correction applied, and key analyses are missing like error taxonomy and FewRel results. R2 acknowledged the useful diagnostic contribution and novel merge amplification finding, though the evaluation metrics could be more granular.\n\nR2's main critique centers on severely inadequate related work—only 6 references when major recent work like LLM-PEE, GoLLIE, and GLiNER are absent—and questions whether the negative findings represent genuine insights or just insufficient method development. The effect sizes are quite small (r=0.156 explaining only 2.4% of variance), and the overall contribution falls below the acceptance threshold. Both reviewers converge that while the multi-channel degradation framework is the strongest piece and merge amplification is intriguing, the related work is drastically lacking and the paper would be better positioned as a Findings paper rather than a full conference submission.\n\nNow I'm loading the review report schema to prepare the consolidated submission.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9871,"cache_read_input_tokens":58053,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9871},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e","uuid":"e8bd0683-0e31-4dc6-b5f4-8481df61a46a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01X76PCMTsdjQ3E9biKE52i2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers completed independently. Now conducting cross-sharing and convergence (Steps 2-3), then producing the synthesis (Step 4).\n\n**Roundtable Summary:**\n- R1 (Methods): 3/Weak Reject — strong experimental design but merge amplification claim overstated, LLaMA null has scale confound, batch size imbalance confound\n- R2 (Novelty): 3/Weak Reject — related work severely inadequate (6 refs), insufficient method development, questionable novelty of negative findings\n\nBoth converge on: the multi-channel diagnostic framework is the strongest contribution; merge amplification is interesting but undersupported; the paper falls below Main threshold but has Findings potential.\n\nLet me load the submission tool and produce the final report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9871,"cache_read_input_tokens":58053,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9871},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a149a487-31d1-4670-8142-477477103f8e","uuid":"098906a0-f958-41a8-acd6-abe7ea95b4e3"}
